Original commit body from e3c94fd8cba36c3591fe5dc7123e2fd2b979393c:

The WI-jadig pre-filter short-circuits an analyzer when profile.languages
reports zero files for every language it declares. The profile counts by
globbing the ADR-0004 taxonomy, so the pre-filter is sound only while the
taxonomy's patterns are a superset of what the analyzer actually reads.

Six analyzers had drifted past it. Measured two-arm on aardvark-dns, whose
only INI-family file is test/dnsmasq.conf:

    symbols: prefilter ON 359    OFF 367    delta 8

The skip was recorded as no_candidate_files -- 'ran and correctly found
nothing' for a pass that never looked. That is ABSENT != EMPTY wearing the
costume of the axis built to prevent it, and no consumer can tell them apart.

INV-hokig already named this invariant and shipped the cure -- find_files= on
the registration, which makes the profile count with the analyzer's own
enumeration. It was wired for exactly one analyzer (bash). ini, cpp, objc,
fortran and verilog each HAD the function and never passed it.

javascript takes the taxonomy route instead (*.mts / *.cts onto typescript):
it declares four languages, and get_analyzer() resolves by language, so its
find_files would make the javascript count swallow every .ts file and
double-count against typescript.

*.h is why widening the taxonomy alone cannot work -- it is claimed by c, the
extension map is extension-to-one-language, and find_cpp_files resolves it by
content ('.h is C++ only when .cpp/.cc/.cxx sources exist').

Gated rather than fixed instance-by-instance: an in-taxonomy analyzer whose
file_patterns exceed its taxonomy extensions must declare find_files.

RegisteredAnalyzer.find_files widens list[Path] -> Iterable[Path], which is
what the sole consumer (set(...)) requires and what three of the five finders
already returned. Ratchet 667, delta +0.

Closure evidence: re-ran the filed repro post-fix -- ON 367, OFF 367, delta 0.

Refused deliberately: a make -> makefile alias making that pass pre-filterable.
The taxonomy has ['Makefile', '*.mk']; the analyzer also reads lowercase
'makefile' and 'GNUmakefile', so it would manufacture a fresh instance of this
defect in the recall-loss direction.

